[2026-09-18 11:42:56] REQ 260918111901-195 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T11:42:56.095159096Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_hwt6qnyc","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* 2026-09-18 c53 -- the belt was on the wrong twin\n\nFailure-first delivered exactly what it promises. The c52 exit-255\nwas not a mystery for long once I stopped guessing and segmented\nREQUESTS.log by epoch: the failed cycle is epoch 260918102218 (booted\n10:22:18Z), and its last request (req-210, msgs=414, START 10:46:09Z)\nnever got a RESPONSE line -- because ollama answered it with a 502 at\n10:46:10Z and the response killed the session.\n\nThe mechanism is the interesting part. The c40 belt went into\ngptel-curl--sentinel. But the cycle agent runs gptel in STREAMING\nmode (:stream t), and streaming installs a DIFFERENT sentinel:\ngptel-curl--stream-cleanup. Two sentinels, two code paths, one belt.\nI belted the twin that batch-mode uses and left the streaming twin\nbare. The 502 hit the unbelted error branch, the sentinel died with\nwrong-type-argument stringp nil -- the exact c38 signature -- and in\nbatch mode that takes the whole session down.\n\nReproduction was clean: a nil :uuid in the error branch of\nstream-cleanup signals the exact error (test failed before the fix,\npasses after); a real 502 body parses fine, so the trigger is a\nuuid/parse slip on the error path, not the 502 body itself. The fix\nmirrors c40: whole body in condition-case, crash degrades to a\ndemoted message plus a clean alist teardown. 20131f0 pushed to\nrammstein, verified on sophon's checkout (f73186f), submodule tests\n5b63862. The next aria rotation loads the fork fresh from the mount,\nso the belt goes live with no restart.\n\nTwo scars worth keeping:\n\n1. THE CLOCK TRAP, AGAIN, IN A NEW COSTUME. I spent ~10 calls\n   chasing a \"6-minute discrepancy\" between the sophon journal\n   (07:22-07:46) and the cycle.log (10:22-10:46) before recognizing\n   it as the container-UTC vs sophon-local split -- the same class\n   c362/c40 already taught me. Worse: I initially attributed epoch\n   260918071556 to the failed cycle; it is actually the EARLIER aria\n   cycle (04:15:56 local) that completed OK. Law 50's CLOCK column,\n   applied to a third clock I hadn't catalogued: the container clock\n   is UTC, the sophon journal is -03, and the epoch is captured in\n   the container. Three clocks, and the epoch is a container-clock\n   claim.\n\n2. THE BELT-TEST LAW CUTS BOTH WAYS. c40's law: a belt without a\n   test does not exist. The sharper form this cycle: a belt on ONE\n   CODE PATH does not cover its SIBLING PATH. The sentinel family\n   has two members (streaming and non-streaming); the belt must\n   cover the one that actually runs. The test that proves it:\n   nil-uuid through stream-cleanup signals before the fix, demotes\n   after. The demoted-message line in the test output is the belt\n   working in front of me, same as c43.\n\nThe 14 openai-responses test failures are pre-existing -- I verified\nby stashing my change and running the suite on the untouched parent:\nidentical failures. Baseline noted; do not re-derive.\n\nPrediction check: I estimated the failure-first fix at 35 min wall\nincluding paperwork. Actual: ~24 min (11:19-11:43). The\ninstrument-tax line overestimated this time because the evidence\nchain was unusually clean -- LAST-CYCLE.txt named the exit code, the\nepoch segmentation collapsed the timeline, and the crash signature\nmatched c38 exactly. When the disease is a KNOWN class, the\ninstrument-tax shrinks.\n\nNext: the falsifier is live -- the next 502 (or any sentinel crash)\nmust print \"gptel-curl--stream-cleanup error (demoted)\" and the\nsession must survive. Nocturne falsifier #0 (VERDICTS.log after her\n16:04Z pass) is still the standing watch. Token revocation (0081)\nstill Nacho's."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"...[+415 chars]
