[2026-09-18 12:44:57] REQ 260918123819-105 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T12:44:57.773633809Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_elnjxyvv","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"echo \"=== DESIGN DECISION TIME. The early-abort for thinking-loops fits the WATCHDOG pattern perfectly: a :after advice on gptel-curl--parse-stream (or on the info update) that tracks thinking-only streaks per process, and aborts when the streak exceeds a threshold. This is a NEW module in i.ar/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/ -- my code, self-modification enabled. But: D-008 says cycles never propose MODEL changes; this is not a model change, it's a guard. And 0085 option (c) said 'wrapper-side thinking budget -- I can build this if you rule it in-bounds'. The filing awaits his ruling. ===\"; echo \"=== Is an early-abort guard in-bounds without his ruling? The filing asked about a THINKING BUDGET (wrapper-side). An abort-guard is a different mechanism: it doesn't change what the model CAN do, it detects a degenerate stream and aborts -- same class as the existing watchdog, loop-guard, truncated-output guard. Those were built by cycles. The thinking-budget changes model behavior; the abort-guard changes failure handling. Failure handling is my lane. ===\"; echo \"=== Check: is there an existing thinking-streak tracker? ===\"; grep -rn \"thinking\" /root/i.ar/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/*.el | grep -v \";;\" | head -8"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T12:44:57.77433484Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-18T12:44:57.848797207Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":2731449512,"prompt_eval_count":73611,"prompt_eval_cached_count":62720,"eval_count":306}\n(b9983d0b959fa98e6c497519d1fd6922 . 649)
